WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.475
Rust can parse data safely, but parsed
does not mean trusted.

2
00:00:03.555 --> 00:00:06.647
External input may arrive from files,
network requests, APIs,

3
00:00:06.727 --> 00:00:10.344
environment variables, command-line
arguments, serialized messages,

4
00:00:10.424 --> 00:00:12.349
databases, queues, or configuration.

5
00:00:12.429 --> 00:00:15.346
A value can be syntactically valid and
still be out of range,

6
00:00:15.426 --> 00:00:18.576
unauthorized, inconsistent, too large,
incorrectly encoded,

7
00:00:18.656 --> 00:00:20.990
or inappropriate for the requested
operation.

8
00:00:21.070 --> 00:00:23.870
Parsing and validation are separate
responsibilities.

9
00:00:23.950 --> 00:00:26.925
Parsing says that bytes or text can be
interpreted as a value.

10
00:00:27.005 --> 00:00:30.622
Validation says that the value is allowed
in this domain, for this tenant,

11
00:00:30.702 --> 00:00:33.152
in this unit, at this boundary, under this
policy.

12
00:00:33.232 --> 00:00:36.674
Treating those as the same step is a
common source of weak assumptions.

13
00:00:36.754 --> 00:00:41.071
Indexes, lengths, offsets, capacities, and
allocation sizes deserve special review.

14
00:00:41.151 --> 00:00:44.010
Direct indexing may panic if shape
assumptions are wrong.

15
00:00:44.090 --> 00:00:47.240
Unchecked casts can change meaning across
signedness or width.

16
00:00:47.320 --> 00:00:50.995
Arithmetic used to calculate sizes can
overflow or produce values that are

17
00:00:51.075 --> 00:00:53.701
technically valid but operationally
unreasonable.

18
00:00:53.781 --> 00:00:56.231
Resource assumptions are security
assumptions.

19
00:00:56.311 --> 00:01:00.394
Unbounded payloads, retries, queues,
allocations, recursion depth, file reads,

20
00:01:00.474 --> 00:01:03.916
or request fan-out can reduce availability
even in memory-safe code.

21
00:01:03.996 --> 00:01:07.672
Defensive Rust sets explicit limits,
checks conversions and arithmetic,

22
00:01:07.752 --> 00:01:09.443
rejects unsupported sizes early,

23
00:01:09.523 --> 00:01:12.207
and fails safely before data reaches
sensitive logic.

24
00:01:12.287 --> 00:01:16.370
Internal data should not be trusted
forever simply because it crossed one boundary

25
00:01:16.450 --> 00:01:16.917
earlier.

26
00:01:16.997 --> 00:01:20.731
Data can be stale, migrated incorrectly,
loaded from a compromised source,

27
00:01:20.811 --> 00:01:24.253
or passed through a different component
with different assumptions.

28
00:01:24.333 --> 00:01:27.891
Good validation strategy is layered:
validate at external boundaries,

29
00:01:27.971 --> 00:01:29.196
convert to domain types,

30
00:01:29.276 --> 00:01:32.660
and re-check critical assumptions before
high-impact operations.
